@*
    One row of a metadata record: a field's name, and whatever the field's values
    draw as.

    A component rather than a near-identical fragment per field inside
    MetadataView. Every field is the same three elements and differs only in
    what goes in the value cell — references become links, plain strings become
    quiet code spans, an estimate becomes a badge, an alias becomes a superscript
    badge, an application key becomes a feature badge — so the row was rebuilt once
    per field and the description-list pairing had a chance per field to be got
    wrong instead of one.

    It always draws the row it is given. Whether there is anything to say is the
    caller's question, because only the caller knows what empty means for its own
    field: an absent list, a blank scalar, and an estimate of zero are three
    different answers, and zero is an estimate that still shows.

    The `dl` around these rows belongs to the record, not here. That is what makes
    a screen reader pair the label with its value, and a row that carried its own
    list would break the pairing for every other row.

    ShowLabel hides the name and does not remove it. Some values say which field
    they came from without help — a points badge, a classification chip, a version
    string with a `v` in front of it — and in front of one of those the label is a
    dimmed word restating what the value already said. What it is not is redundant
    to a screen reader: a value read out with no field name is worse to hear than a
    name nobody needed to see. So the `dt` stays, wearing `sr-only`, and the row
    takes a `--bare` modifier off whatever `BaseClass` it was given so the
    stylesheet can give the value the room the hidden label is no longer holding.

    The indentation in front of <dd> is load-bearing: Razor emits the whitespace
    between two sibling elements as content, so those twenty columns are part of
    the markup every knowledge record has always rendered. Reflowing the line is a
    markup change, not a formatting one.
*@

<div class="@RowClass">
    <dt class="@LabelClass">@Label</dt>
                    <dd class="@ValueCssClass" data-testid="@TestId">@ChildContent</dd>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The field's name, drawn as written. A knowledge field's name is
    /// already the reader's own word for it — <c>depends-on</c> is what the file
    /// says — so there is nothing to translate.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Label { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The value cell's contents. A fragment rather than a string
    /// because what a value looks like is the whole difference between the
    /// fields: a link, a code span, a badge, a chip.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The class on the row. Null drops it, for a host laying the pair
    /// out with its own grid.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? BaseClass { get; set; } = "knowledge-fields__row";

    /// <summary>A modifier on top of <see cref="BaseClass"/>, for a row that is
    /// drawn differently from its siblings.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? LabelCssClass { get; set; } = "knowledge-fields__label";

    [Parameter]
    public string? ValueCssClass { get; set; } = "knowledge-fields__value";

    /// <summary>A test id on the value cell, where the values are. Absent on most
    /// rows: a row is found by its label, and only the fields whose values are
    /// drawn as something other than text — the key badges — have needed one of
    /// their own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the field's name is drawn where a reader can see it. Off
    /// for a value that names its own field; the name is still in the markup and
    /// still announced either way.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowLabel { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>The class on the row: the base, the <c>--bare</c> modifier when the
    /// label is hidden, then the caller's own. The modifier is derived from
    /// <see cref="BaseClass"/> rather than hard-coded, so a host laying these out
    /// under its own names gets a modifier in its own namespace instead of one of
    /// ours.</summary>
    private string? RowClass
    {
        get
        {
            var bare = ShowLabel || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(BaseClass) ? null : $"{BaseClass}--bare";

            return string.Join(' ', new[] { BaseClass, bare, CssClass }
                .Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part))) is { Length: > 0 } value
                ? value
                : null;
        }
    }

    private string? LabelClass =>
        ShowLabel ? LabelCssClass
        : string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(LabelCssClass) ? "sr-only"
        : $"{LabelCssClass} sr-only";
}
